  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday Night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1144 PM CDT Wed Jun 17 2026\n\nThe front should settle down into the Red River Valley by Thursday \nevening, and all models suggest a pretty unsettled night across \nsouthern OK that night. The potential for slow-moving or training \nstorms is higher during this time frame, increasing the threat for \nheavy rainfall and flash flooding. What happens as we go into Friday \nis more uncertain. Some data has an MCS organizing Thursday night \nand either tracking along or south of the Red River region into \nFriday. The latest HRRR tracks this MCS east across a good portion \nof our area Friday, while the GFS takes more it into TX. The \nboundary lifts back north Saturday ahead of another shortwave trough \nmoving east over the central Rockies and into the Plains. While \nshower and storm coverage does not look widespread, you can expect \nsome rain/storm chances. As the shortwave trough and associated \nsurface low progresses east over the central Plains Saturday night \ninto Sunday, a strong to severe MCS could progress southeast from \nthe High Plains and across the northern portions of the forecast \narea (NE OK/NW AR). A cold front will settle down into the region \nSunday afternoon and night, with increasing shower and storm chances \nthat will likely linger into Monday. This looks like another window \nwhere locally heavy rainfall that could lead to flash flooding will \nbe a higher threat.\n\nLacy",
